{
  "_comment": "Hinglish comprehension bake-off — plan.md §9, Phase 5b checkpoint 3.",
  "_why": "Fine-tuning teaches format and voice cheaply. It does not cheaply teach a language the base model does not already know. So the base model is chosen on one question: can it read Roman-script code-mixed Hindi and extract the clinical facts? Everything else the fine-tune can fix.",
  "_method": "Each sample is prompted zero-shot with: 'Render this patient note in English. Do not add anything.' The output is then scanned for each expected fact. A fact counts as extracted if any of its accepted surface forms appears. This is keyword recall, which is crude — but the question here is comprehension, not phrasing, and a model that renders the fact in any reasonable English wording passes.",
  "_scoring": "Per-model score = extracted facts / total facts. Also reported: samples with a hallucinated negation (see `mustNotContain`), which is the failure that matters clinically — a model that turns 'dard hai' into 'no pain' is unusable at any recall.",

